+# Decision: 2Q Eviction Policy for Preview Cache
+
+**Status**: Accepted
+**Date**: 2026-10-18
+**Segment**: previews
+**Related specs**: PREVIEW-006 through PREVIEW-011
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker maintains a private 12 GiB cache of rendered JPEG contact sheets. Workloads alternate between:
+- Long sequential screenings (many one-use sheets)
+- Comparison sessions (repeated access to a small working set)
+
+The eviction policy must protect comparison sheets during screenings while adapting when editors switch projects. Three candidates were evaluated.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+### Option A: LRU (Least Recently Used)
+- **Mechanism**: Single queue, evict tail on space pressure
+- **Bookkeeping**: Minimal (one timestamp or list position per entry)
+- **Scan resistance**: Poor — a long screening evicts the comparison working set
+- **Project-switch adaptation**: Fast — old project entries age out quickly
+- **Comparison-sheet retention**: Weak — no distinction between one-use and reused
+
+### Option B: Frequency-Based with Aging
+- **Mechanism**: Access counter per entry, periodic decay (e.g., halve counters every N minutes)
+- **Bookkeeping**: Counter per entry + background aging job
+- **Scan resistance**: Moderate — one-use sheets stay at count=1, but aging slowly promotes them
+- **Project-switch adaptation**: Slow — yesterday's favorites retain high counts until aging catches up
+- **Comparison-sheet retention**: Strong for repeatedly popular clips
+
+### Option C: 2Q (Two Queues) — **Selected**
+- **Mechanism**: Two LRU queues (A1 for first-use, Am for promoted entries). New entries enter A1 head. On second access, promote to Am head. Evict A1 tail first, then Am tail.
+- **Bookkeeping**: Two queues + queue membership per entry (no counters, no aging job)
+- **Scan resistance**: Strong — one-use entries stay in A1 and are evicted first
+- **Project-switch adaptation**: Moderate — A1 turns over fast; Am retains promoted entries until displaced
+- **Comparison-sheet retention**: Strong — promoted entries live in Am with 75% of budget
+
+## Decision Criteria
+
+| Criterion | Weight | LRU | Frequency+Aging | 2Q |
+|-----------|--------|-----|-----------------|-----|
+| Scan resistance (protect comparison sheets) | High | ❌ | ⚠️ | ✅ |
+| Bookkeeping complexity | Medium | ✅ | ❌ | ⚠️ |
+| Project-switch adaptation | Medium | ✅ | ❌ | ⚠️ |
+| Comparison-sheet retention | High | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
+| Implementation risk | Low | ✅ | ❌ | ⚠️ |
+
+## Selection
+
+**2Q (Option C)** selected. The 25%/75% A1/Am split (3 GiB / 9 GiB) reserves the majority of cache for promoted entries while giving sequential scans a bounded buffer. All three policies keep the external HTTP interface, render queue concurrency (4), and 12 GiB disk budget unchanged — the decision is internal to the preview worker.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- **Positive**: Comparison sheets survive long screenings; no background aging thread; simpler than frequency+aging.
+- **Negative**: Two queues to manage; queue-size split (25/75) is a tuning parameter; project-switch lag slightly worse than pure LRU (Am entries persist).
+- **Follow-on**: PREVIEW-006 through PREVIEW-011 encode the 2Q mechanics in specs.
+
+## Rejected Alternatives
+
+- **LRU**: Rejected because it fails the primary goal — protecting comparison sheets during screenings.
+- **Frequency+Aging**: Rejected due to counter maintenance overhead and slow project-switch adaptation; the aging job adds operational complexity.
